# Cloudflare Worker esbuild bundle (deploy-time artifact; ADR 032 / P042).
# Generated by `npm run build:worker` before terraform; never committed so
# the deployed bundle is always derived fresh from the tested source.
# REPOINTED TWICE ON 2026-08-10. Every rule below carries a mid-pattern
# separator, which anchors it to THIS FILE'S directory -- the repo root. When
# `deploy/` became `packages/deployment/` (bf106786) the whole block silently
# stopped matching, and the cleartext-secret protection noted at the tfplan
# rules went with it. It was repointed again the same day when
# `packages/deployment/` became `apps/addressr-deployment/`.
#
# A directory move strips anchored ignore rules with no error and no diff. Two
# strips in one day is the evidence: this is a recurring failure, not a one-off.
# `deploy-artefact-ignores.test.mjs` now makes the third one LOUD -- it runs
# `git check-ignore` against the real rules, so a stripped rule reds the build
# instead of quietly exposing tfplan.json in a public repo.
apps/addressr-deployment/cloudflare-worker/worker.bundled.js

# next.js build output
.next
@cucumber-*.rerun

test-results
target

lib
apps/addressr-deployment/resolved_*
apps/addressr-deployment/task-definitions/addressr-server.json
apps/addressr-deployment/plan.out
# Terraform plan artifacts (deploy:plan / terraform-plan.yml). BOTH the binary
# plan and its JSON rendering contain every root variable value in CLEARTEXT,
# including TF_VAR_aws_secret_key, the ADR-024 proxy-auth secret and the
# Cloudflare token — `sensitive = true` flags them, it does not redact them.
# This repo is public; see RISK-POLICY.md "Confidential Information".
apps/addressr-deployment/tfplan
apps/addressr-deployment/tfplan.json
apps/addressr-deployment/.terraform
**/terraform.tfstate
**/*.auto.tfvars


